WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.320
Which Avenger should've had a costume all along,&nbsp;
and which Avenger has the very best costume in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.640
the entire MCU? Keep watching for a look at some&nbsp;
unpopular opinions about Marvel's movie costumes.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.800
After a quick cameo as a SHIELD agent in 2011's&nbsp;
"Thor," Clint Barton would make his real debut as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.240
Hawkeye in "The Avengers," only to spend the first&nbsp;
half of the movie under the thrall of Loki and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.120
the Mind Stone in his scepter. He's freed from&nbsp;
mind control in time to join the other heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.920
in fighting off the invasion of New York by the&nbsp;
Chitauri, establishing him as a member of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.000
Avengers going forward. Unfortunately, he never&nbsp;
actually looks the slightest bit like a superhero.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.040
The classic Hawkeye of Marvel Comics originally&nbsp;
wore a purple and blue costume with a pointy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.920
Wolverine-style mask. The Hawkeye of the movie&nbsp;
owes more to the version from Marvel's Ultimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.240
Universe, but even that character is often&nbsp;
depicted with a more distinctive costume than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.520
movie version. In "The Avengers," Clint Barton&nbsp;
basically just wears a tactical vest, pants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.760
and combat boots. Sure, when he's with SHIELD at&nbsp;
the beginning and then taken over by Loki, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.640
outfit makes sense. But after that scene between&nbsp;
him and Black Widow on the SHIELD helicarrier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.640
when he's just been freed from the mind control&nbsp;
and declares that he's joining the fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.640
wouldn't it have been cool if he'd&nbsp;
put on a new outfit to reflect that?

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.520
Years later, the "Hawkeye" series would make a&nbsp;
plot point out of the blandness of his costume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.160
Clint Barton insists that his job is done&nbsp;
better if he doesn't call attention to himself;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.040
Kate Bishop insists he has a branding problem.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.000
"How about enough materials to&nbsp;
make your costumes plus two more?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.240
I know a certain someone who&nbsp;
needs a little help with theirs."

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.320
While he shouldn't have worn buccaneer&nbsp;
boots and a loincloth over his pants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.480
something in blue and purple with a mask&nbsp;
or helmet would have been a nice touch.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.400
The outfit Scott Lang wears as Ant-Man is based&nbsp;
very closely on the classic Ant-Man costumes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:58.160
worn by both Lang and Hank Pym in the comics.&nbsp;
However, the comics version always shows some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:04.000
the character's face. In the movies, Scott's face&nbsp;
is mostly hidden, which makes Ant-Man look oddly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.400
alien and even creepy, which is especially strange&nbsp;
for such a humorous, down-to-Earth character.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.400
While it might be a bit more of a&nbsp;
challenge for the digital effects artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.280
Ant-Man would work better if he had a human&nbsp;
face visible in his heroic identity. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.800
it would be Paul Rudd's face, which&nbsp;
is not only one of the most handsome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.800
faces in movies, but also one of the&nbsp;
most comically expressive as well.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.760
In the early days of the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;
Universe, everyone seemed much more reluctant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.280
to let superhero movies be as colorful as comics&nbsp;
have always been. With Hawkeye in a tactical vest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.240
Black Widow in black leather, and even Captain&nbsp;
America's patriotic colors somewhat muted, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:48.960
palette felt oddly limited. Then came "Avengers:&nbsp;
Age of Ultron," and the debut of the Vision.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.240
"Age of Ultron" might be one of the least-beloved&nbsp;
MCU movies, but Paul Bettany's evolution from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.760
disembodied artificial intelligence to powerful&nbsp;
android Avenger is one of the best things in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.200
it. From the moment the Vision first appears, he&nbsp;
looks like he stepped out of a comic book panel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.280
in a good way. With his green outfit, yellow&nbsp;
cape, and crimson face, he shows that comics-style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.760
color schemes don't have to be toned down&nbsp;
or discarded for live action adaptations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:21.520
For those viewers who really love superheroes&nbsp;
and their costumes, it was a huge step forward.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.960
There was a lot of great stuff in the Disney+&nbsp;
"Loki" series, but there were some missteps too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.280
The introduction of the Time Variance Authority&nbsp;
was a perfect follow-up to the reality-breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.560
events of "Avengers: Endgame," with alternate&nbsp;
timelines becoming as much a part of the MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.040
as they are of the Marvel Comics Multiverse.&nbsp;
Getting to meet Sylvie and the other alternate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.920
Lokis was also a lot of fun. However,&nbsp;
that's nothing compared to what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.640
done to Loki by the end of the season,&nbsp;
and costuming was a big part of that.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.800
Due to the events of the show, Loki ends&nbsp;
up wearing a TVA uniform: windbreaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.000
dress shirt, and skinny tie. That makes for an&nbsp;
interesting contrast with his usual green and gold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.320
space-god garb, but it has uncomfortable&nbsp;
implications in the context of the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.720
the series, when Loki begins to think he&nbsp;
and Sylvie may be going too far and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.840
He Who Remains and the TVA may be right&nbsp;
about the need to control the timeline.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.000
This is Loki, after all: the god of&nbsp;
mischief and chaos. Anyone who's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.640
fan of Loki loves him because of that&nbsp;
mischief and chaos, so nobody wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.840
see him as a guy in a brown jacket and&nbsp;
tie, arguing in favor of law and order.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.400
"Watch it! Where's your manners? Hey, quit it!"

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.640
In general, the costume Chadwick Boseman wore&nbsp;
as Black Panther is great. It's also quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.560
close to his comic book outfit, albeit with&nbsp;
the kind of detailing and texture that works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.280
better in a movie. However, the Marvel Comics&nbsp;
version of T'Challa often, but not always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.240
wears a flowing black cape with his costume,&nbsp;
giving him something of a Batman look. For a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:05:00.240
superhero who's also a king, it makes him seem a&nbsp;
bit more regal than your average Marvel brawler.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.880
Obviously, the Black Panther&nbsp;
couldn't wear a cape all the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.080
but when he was acting in the role of&nbsp;
Wakanda's ruler, it would have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.080
cool to see Boseman in the cape. Plus,&nbsp;
if it was part of the high-tech costume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.200
given to T'Challa by his genius sister Shuri,&nbsp;
the cape could probably retract on command,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.760
so Black Panther could be ready quickly for&nbsp;
situations that require more freedom of movement.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.720
Although there are variations throughout the&nbsp;
franchise, the main costume Chris Evans wears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.320
as Captain America is pretty much perfect.&nbsp;
It takes his classic design and colors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:35.600
adds some realistic-looking details that make&nbsp;
the suit look more tactical and less costumey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.800
There's only one quibble with the outfit, and&nbsp;
that's how often Steve takes off the helmet.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.600
Leaving aside the fact that Cap's not invincible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.840
and that helmet presumably provides&nbsp;
some cranial protection, not wearing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:52.240
also makes him feel a little less like the&nbsp;
Star-Spangled Avenger he's supposed to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:57.040
Going around unmasked and unhelmeted&nbsp;
feels less like a character-based choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.600
and more like the filmmakers being concerned&nbsp;
that fans need to see Chris Evans' handsome face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.120
as much as possible. While it's true that MCU&nbsp;
Steve Rogers has no secret identity to protect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:12.400
the comics version of the character does, and&nbsp;
that mask with the "A" is still an iconic look.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.680
After Steve Rogers decides in "Avengers: Endgame"&nbsp;
to live out his life in an alternate past with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.280
Peggy Carter, the government chooses&nbsp;
a soldier named John Walker to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.200
the new Captain America. This is a story&nbsp;
that also happened, with a few changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.360
in the comics, but a big difference is that&nbsp;
when Walker was Captain America in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.240
he wore the exact same costume Steve had worn.&nbsp;
For "The Falcon and the Winter Soldier," they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:40.240
dressed Walker in a similar but distinct outfit&nbsp;
that hints at his later identity as USAgent.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.320
The problem with this is that the whole&nbsp;
John Walker story is about this guy who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.040
isn't as capable or stable as Steve&nbsp;
Rogers tarnishing the reputation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.760
the very idea of Captain America. The TV&nbsp;
series does a good job of telling that story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:59.760
but symbolically it would be a lot more effective&nbsp;
if he wore the classic Captain America costume.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:07.840
No character's costume in the MCU balances all of&nbsp;
the needs of live-action adaptation from comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:13.520
better than the distinctive garb worn by&nbsp;
Doctor Strange. The blue tunic and red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:17.360
cloak are immediately recognizable from&nbsp;
the classic version of the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.400
but with realistic detailing and texture&nbsp;
in place of a big blue trident design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.480
and leggings. In addition to his Cloak of&nbsp;
Levitation — which still has a high collar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:32.000
but a less ridiculous one than the comics version&nbsp;
— he has his big amulet, the Eye of Agamotto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:37.680
from which he gets much of his power. Even beyond&nbsp;
his resemblance to the classic Steve Ditko design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:43.840
Strange looks like a modern-day wizard,&nbsp;
which is exactly what he's meant to be.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.400
"Dude, you're embarrassing&nbsp;
me in front of the wizards."

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.880
Spider-Man's arrival on the scene in "Captain&nbsp;
America: Civil War" was a game-changer for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.160
the MCU, and also for live action depictions&nbsp;
of Spider-Man. After Tony Stark shows up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:03.280
Queens to recruit Peter Parker, Spider-Man debuts&nbsp;
during the movie's big superhero confrontation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:07.200
appearing as if from nowhere to catch&nbsp;
Captain America's shield out of the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:12.800
dressed in a costume made for him by&nbsp;
Stark. Then something amazing happens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:17.440
the eyes on Spider-Man's mask change&nbsp;
size to focus on what he's looking at.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.560
Spider-Man is one of the few classic superheroes&nbsp;
whose costume has always covered his entire face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.880
This can create a challenge for artists,&nbsp;
because he still needs to be able to express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.440
emotion. For as long as he's existed, the&nbsp;
standard way of dealing with this problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:37.120
is to depict the oversized eyes of his mask&nbsp;
moving and changing size as if they're real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.240
eyes—getting bigger for surprise, narrower for&nbsp;
anger and so forth. For obvious reasons, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:47.120
nobody had ever attempted to depict&nbsp;
that in live action, until the MCU.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.520
The excuse that his costume is a high-tech Stark&nbsp;
creation, combined with a line or two about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:57.040
how light filtration helps Spider-Man focus his&nbsp;
overactive super-senses, enables his mask to be as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.640
expressive as it's always been on the page — and&nbsp;
that allows for a friendly neighborhood Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:07.840
who is far more human and relatable when he's&nbsp;
in costume than any previous incarnation.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:14.160
Spider-Man's MCU costume is pretty much perfect,&nbsp;
but his first couple of supervillains have left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:19.120
something to be desired. The main villain of&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: Homecoming" is the Vulture, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:24.480
Shocker is also there as a secondary antagonist&nbsp;
in the Vulture's employ. Unfortunately neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:29.360
character really wears a costume, just a mix of&nbsp;
streetwear and high-tech gadgetry that vaguely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:33.360
evokes each character's classic look. It's&nbsp;
one thing for the Vulture to have metal wings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.320
but it's kind of a shame to put that metal helmet&nbsp;
with the glowing eyes on him, when just shaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.880
Michael Keaton's head would really highlight&nbsp;
his resemblance to the Marvel Comics Vulture.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:49.040
The MCU Vulture and Shocker are menacing, but&nbsp;
they're just not as iconic as they ought to be.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:53.680
Fortunately, the second movie fixes that&nbsp;
problem. As Mysterio, Jake Gyllenhaal wears a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:57.440
visually striking version of the&nbsp;
supervillain's classic bubble-headed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:03.434
green costume, which turns out to be just as&nbsp;
special-effects-generated as his superpowers.

00:10:03.434 --> 00:10:05.760
In his first few cinematic appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:10.960
Thor wore a version of his classic Asgardian garb&nbsp;
from the comics: black leather armor inset with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:16.560
metal discs, a red cape, and even a winged helmet&nbsp;
in some shots, although the widespread fear of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:21.200
letting superheroes look silly on film keeps&nbsp;
him from wearing the helmet for long. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.400
with the long blond hair and hammer, he's&nbsp;
instantly recognizable as the comics character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:31.760
On the other hand, the level of detailing on his&nbsp;
armor is a bit much, and it misses the "Jack Kirby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:37.440
but real" aesthetic it seems to be aiming for,&nbsp;
landing somewhere in "Lord of the Rings" knockoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:42.240
territory. For the early MCU, it was fine,&nbsp;
but it was never anything to write home about.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.400
Then "Thor: Ragnarok" came along,&nbsp;
and all of a sudden Thor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:51.760
cool. Stripped of his hammer and exiled from&nbsp;
Asgard by his estranged older sister Hela,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.320
Thor becomes a gladiator on the planet&nbsp;
Sakaar. He gets a haircut, from Stan Lee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:02.640
no less, and a new outfit, and it's totally a&nbsp;
glow-up. No longer is Thor a cosmic prince in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.600
ceremonial-looking armor. Now he looks&nbsp;
like the badass fighter he actually is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:13.600
with simpler leather armor, cool war paint, and&nbsp;
only one pauldron, a look that always works.

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:18.320
Of course, his appearance continues to evolve,&nbsp;
and he'll definitely need another makeover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:22.320
after sinking into depression in "Avengers:&nbsp;
Endgame." However, since his next appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:26.320
will be in "Thor: Love and Thunder," which&nbsp;
is directed by Taika Waititi with costume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.600
designs by "Ragnarok" designer Mayes Rubeo,&nbsp;
we can trust that he'll once again look great.

